<h1>Source vs generated</h1>
<p>A DemiEngine project contains three kinds of data, and knowing which is which tells you what to edit, what to commit, and what to ignore. This page defines the three buckets, maps them onto the project's folders, and explains why game saves deliberately live outside the project tree.</p>

<h2>The three buckets</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Source</strong> — the documents and files you author: scenes, prefabs, scripts, asset manifests and their sources, game data, and project files. These are the game. You edit them, you review them, you commit them.</li>
  <li><strong>Generated</strong> — output produced by tools from source: cook output under <code>generated/</code>, installed package contents under <code>.demi/</code>, and derived geometry such as fracture data. Never hand-edit these; regenerate them instead.</li>
  <li><strong>Writable</strong> — data the running game creates for the player: saves and caches. These live in the platform user-data and cache directories and never inside the project.</li>
</ul>

