<h2>What to commit and what to ignore</h2>
<p>Commit every source file. Reviews then show real design changes — a moved entity, a new asset, a retuned value — as small diffs in readable JSON.</p>
<p>Ignore generated and writable paths in version control. They are reproducible from source or per-player, so committing them would only add noise and merge conflicts. Typical ignore rules cover <code>generated/</code>, <code>.demi/</code>, build output directories, and packaged artifacts such as <code>*.apk</code>, <code>*.aab</code>, and cooked bundles.</p>
<p>Two habits keep the split clean:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Never hand-edit anything under <code>generated/</code> or <code>.demi/</code>. Your changes would be overwritten the next time a tool runs. Fix the source and let the tool produce new output.</li>
  <li>Never write gameplay content into generated folders. If a tool seems to want that, the content belongs in <code>scenes/</code>, <code>data/</code>, or <code>assets/</code> first.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why saves never live in the project tree</h2>
<p>Saves are per-player runtime data, not game content. They are written to the platform user-data directory — on Linux <code>$XDG_DATA_HOME/&lt;game&gt;</code>, falling back to the usual per-user data location — and caches use the matching cache directory. Keeping them out of the project tree matters for three reasons:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Updates stay safe</strong> — installing a new build of the game replaces content, not player progress. Saves that lived beside the content would be overwritten or deleted with it.</li>
  <li><strong>Packaging stays honest</strong> — a shipped package must contain the audited game content and nothing else. Player files mixed into the source tree risk being packed into a release or blocked by the content audit.</li>
  <li><strong>Multiple users stay separate</strong> — two accounts on one machine must not share or clobber one another's progress. Per-user directories give each player their own slots.</li>
</ul>
<p>Read and write slots through the save API rather than raw file paths; that is what routes them to the right directory on every platform. See <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'data-and-save-api'}) }}">Data &amp; save API</a>. Save document shape and migrations are described with the other formats in <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'project-format'}) }}">Project &amp; file formats</a>.</p>

<h2>How cooking creates generated/</h2>
<p>Cooking is the step that turns source into runtime-ready content. You ask for a platform, and the engine validates the project and every reachable source, then writes cooked output under <code>generated/cooked/&lt;platform&gt;</code> together with a <code>cook.manifest.json</code> listing every included path and hash:</p>
<pre><code>demi cook --project games/my_game --platform linux</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>cook</code> — validates and cooks platform content.</li>
  <li><code>--project games/my_game</code> — the project to cook.</li>
  <li><code>--platform linux</code> — which platform to cook for. Output lands under <code>generated/cooked/linux</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The manifest's hashes make the output checkable and rebuilds incremental: content that did not change is not redone. That is also why you never edit cooked files — they are derived from source by a deterministic step, and the next cook would replace them. The pipeline and hash inputs are described in <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'cooking'}) }}">Cooking</a>.</p>
<p>Release builds reuse the same cooked content. <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'shipping'}) }}">Shipping &amp; builds</a> covers packaging a cooked project into a Linux bundle or an Android APK/AAB, and the <a href="{{ path('docs_page', {slug: 'cli-build-and-test'}) }}">Build, cook &amp; test</a> page lists every related command.</p>

